A library system publishes notifications when a reserved book is returned to many independent patron devices. The system keeps a list of registered listeners and calls them in a loop:

```java
public void publish(Notification n) {
    for (Listener listener : listeners) {
        listener.onNotify(n);
    }
}
```

Which statements about this design are correct?

A `ReturnedBooksRegistry` class in a library application is written like this and is read from every request handler:

```java
public class ReturnedBooksRegistry {
    private static ReturnedBooksRegistry instance;
    private ReturnedBooksRegistry() { }
    public static ReturnedBooksRegistry getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new ReturnedBooksRegistry();
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

Which criticisms of this code are justified?

An overdue notice generator fixes the order of the steps and lets subclasses vary two of them:

```java
public abstract class NoticeGenerator {
    public final Notice generate() {
        Data data = load();
        Data shaped = transform(data);
        return write(render(shaped));
    }
    protected Data load() { return Data.fromDefaultSource(); }
    protected abstract Data transform(Data data);
    protected abstract String render(Data data);
}
```

Which statements about this design are correct?

A library system processes returned books with interchangeable algorithms:

```java
public interface ReturnStrategy { void process(List<Book> returns); }
public class SimpleReturn implements ReturnStrategy { ... }
public class ComplexReturn  implements ReturnStrategy { ... }
public class ReturnProcessor { private List<Book> books; private ReturnStrategy returnAlgorithm; ... }
public class ReturnPolicy  { private ReturnProcessor processor; public void configure() { ... } }
```

Using the class names from this listing: the class that holds the books and a reference to the currently selected algorithm is [-spot 1]. The type that both `SimpleReturn` and `ComplexReturn` implement is [-spot 2]. The class whose `configure()` method decides which algorithm to select is [-spot 3].

A reviewer examines the following class in a library system:

```java
public class NotificationService {
    private final SmtpMailer mailer = new SmtpMailer("mail.internal", 25);
    public void sendNotification(User user, String message) { /* ... */ mailer.send(user.email(), message); }
}
```

The reviewer's first objection is that `NotificationService` names a concrete low-level class instead of an abstraction such as a `Notifier` interface; the principle being violated is abbreviated [-spot 1] (three letters). The reviewer's fix is to stop constructing the collaborator inside the class and supply it from outside instead — the general two‑word name for that technique is [-spot 2].
